765625, 0.051910400390625, 0.0430908203125, 0.033233642578125, 0.0222930908203125, 0.00804901123046875, -0.0137939453125, -0.042022705078125, 0.004444122314453125, 0.01385498046875, 0.0125885009765625, -0.01126861572265625, -0.0090179443359375, 0.0062713623046875, -0.019622802734375, -0.0138702392578125, -0.00807952880859375, 0.002643585205078125, 0.036895751953125, -0.00856781005859375, -0.00794219970703125, -0.0188446044921875, -0.01739501953125, 0.005611419677734375, -0.0277099609375, 0.019317626953125, -0.0081329345703125, 0.015411376953125, -0.0006108283996582031, -0.039825439453125, -0.0059356689453125, 0.003993988037109375, 0.0009036064147949219, -0.026702880859375, -0.0113067626953125, 0.04010009765625, -0.01369476318359375, -0.002719879150390625, -0.0272369384765625, -0.007480621337890625, 0.0323486328125, 0.002826690673828125, 0.029876708984375, 0.0173492431640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, with a particular focus on electrical circuits. A central component of this approach involves transforming the ordinary differential equations governing system behavior from the real domain into the Laplace domain. Achieving this transformation necessitates a well-defined Laplace transform within the context of GA. In this work, we extend previous research that generalized the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras by providing a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored to Geometric Algebra. Our formulation and its associated properties are valid for geometric algebras with signature up to and including 5."}}